{"chunk_id": "d239e78f44cf", "text": "During the night that Shatrughna stays at Valmiki\u2019s forest hermitage, Sita gives birth to twin sons. Young ascetics inform the sage Valmiki, who immediately visits the newborns, marveling at their radiance and performing protective rites to ward off evil forces. Using sacred kusa grass and mantras, he conducts a ritual purification and naming ceremony. The first child, touched with consecrated kusa grass, is named Kusha; the second, dried with its roots by the women ascetics, is named Lava. The hermitage community carefully completes the protective rites. When Shatrughna hears the joyful news, he approaches Sita\u2019s hut and offers blessings. This quiet but pivotal moment introduces Kusha and Lava, the future heirs of Rama and the transmitters of the Ramayana itself.\nIn Valmiki\u2019s hermitage, Sita gives birth to twin sons; Valmiki performs protective rites and names them Kusha and Lava.\nThemes: birth of heirs, ascetic hermitage life, naming ritual, divine lineage", "vector": [0.03039616160094738, 0.004639378748834133, 0.00668527465313673, -0.018020005896687508, -0.008633746765553951, 0.028004245832562447, -0.021540693938732147, -0.00991033110767603, -0.016488103196024895, -0.023677293211221695, 0.013464612886309624, 0.009863299317657948, -0.001851048320531845, 0.00876812357455492, -0.0046494570560753345, -0.0037155344616621733, 0.04361889511346817, -0.006960748229175806, 0.02305915765464306, -0.011113009415566921, -0.004558752290904522, -0.011684113182127476, 0.0006231750594452024, 0.010817378759384155, -0.017912503331899643, -0.0022239454556256533, 0.021245062351226807, -0.013296641409397125, 0.0022676181979477406, -0.016622481867671013, -0.025021066889166832, -0.019430968910455704, -0.005401970352977514, -0.009574388153851032, -0.04087759926915169, -0.008902501314878464, 0.005018995143473148, -0.013175701722502708, 0.018718767911195755, 0.0019803866744041443, 0.009265320375561714, -0.015050265938043594, -0.00898984